Q: Appium: autoAcceptAlert doesn&#39;t work to allow access to all photos on IOS 14 A: <p>Following up on this, as it is still a problem for us with Appium 2 usage.</p>
<p>I was able to address this by switching app context to springboard, then selecting the popup/alert, then switching back to my app.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export async function executeInSpringboard(
    fn: () =&gt; Promise&lt;void&gt;
): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    if (platform === MobilePlatform.IOS) {
        await driver.executeScript('mobile: activateApp', [
            { bundleId: 'com.apple.springboard' },
        ]);
    }
    await driver.pause(500);
    await fn();
    if (platform === MobilePlatform.IOS) {
        await driver.executeScript('mobile: activateApp', [
            { bundleId: packageName },  /* example: com.website.AppName */
        ]);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: read stdin in function in bash script A: <p>The first command that &quot;takes STDIN but no command is piped to it&quot;, receives the STDIN from the function/script itself, but it happens only once and the next command without pipe, receives empty STDIN.</p>
<p>In the below example, <code>cat</code> is a command that takes STDIN. After the first <code>echo</code>, it takes it from the inline pipe and after the second <code>echo</code>, it takes it from the function/script (only once), therefore it takes empty after the third <code>echo</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>function stdTest {
  echo 1:; echo &quot;Local STDIN&quot; | cat -n;
  echo 2:; cat -n;
  echo 3:; cat -n;
}


$ echo -e &quot;Hey\nYou&quot; | stdTest

Result:
1:
     1  Local STDIN
2:
     1  Hey
     2  You
3:
</code></pre>
